    },
    {
      "bullet": "Elevated household leverage and commercial real estate exposures persist as key vulnerabilities, despite overall bank nonperforming loan ratios staying below 0.6 percent.",
      "section_ids": ["S01", "S06", "S10", "S13"]
    },
    {
      "bullet": "Economic growth risks are tilted to the downside, where a renewed escalation of trade tensions could reduce real GDP by about 0.75 percent below baseline by Q3:2026.",
      "section_ids": ["S07", "S09", "S13"]
    },
    {
      "bullet": "Housing affordability pressures remain acute across major urban centers, underpinned by high borrowing costs and a structural supply shortfall estimated at 2.5 million units by 2035.",
      "section_ids": ["S11"]
    },
    {
      "bullet": "General government deficits of about 2 to 2.5 percent of GDP in 2025–26 provide appropriate countercyclical support while preserving fiscal space under moderate public debt levels.",
      "section_ids": ["S09", "S13"]
    },
    {
      "bullet": "Structural productivity weakness remains a key vulnerability, highlighted by Canadian output per hour worked falling roughly 30 percent below that of the United States.",
      "section_ids": ["S01", "S12", "S13"]
    },
    {
      "bullet": "Eliminating non-geographic internal trade barriers—which average about 9.5 percent tariff equivalents—could raise Canada's real GDP by roughly 7 percent over the long run.",
      "section_ids": ["S12", "S19"]
    }
  ]
}

